1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,002
Episode Forty-Six showed CompletableFuture on the common pool.

2
00:00:04,002 --> 00:00:08,585
That pool is a ForkJoinPool — built for divide-and-conquer parallelism.

3
00:00:08,585 --> 00:00:12,523
Workers split big tasks into smaller pieces and join results.

4
00:00:12,523 --> 00:00:16,396
Idle threads steal work from busy neighbors — work-stealing.

5
00:00:16,396 --> 00:00:20,528
RecursiveTask and RecursiveAction model fork-join decomposition.

6
00:00:20,528 --> 00:00:25,498
Today — ForkJoinPool, work-stealing, and when recursive parallelism pays off.

7
00:00:25,498 --> 00:00:27,422
Episode Forty-Seven.

8
00:00:27,422 --> 00:00:30,404
ForkJoinPool and Work-Stealing.

9
00:00:30,404 --> 00:00:34,865
ForkJoinPool is an ExecutorService tuned for parallel decomposition.

10
00:00:34,865 --> 00:00:39,195
fork splits a task into subtasks — join waits for subtask results.

11
00:00:39,195 --> 00:00:42,869
invoke runs a single task and blocks until it completes.

12
00:00:42,869 --> 00:00:46,740
submit returns a ForkJoinTask — use when you need a handle.

13
00:00:46,740 --> 00:00:51,201
Pool size defaults to available processors — tune for your workload.

14
00:00:51,201 --> 00:00:54,678
Think tree-shaped computation — split down, merge up.

15
00:00:54,678 --> 00:00:58,803
Each worker maintains a deque of tasks — LIFO for its own work.

16
00:00:58,803 --> 00:01:03,452
When a worker runs dry, it steals from another worker deque — FIFO end.

17
00:01:03,452 --> 00:01:07,642
Stealing balances load without a central coordinator bottleneck.

18
00:01:07,642 --> 00:01:12,290
Fine-grained tasks keep workers busy — coarse tasks leave threads idle.

19
00:01:12,290 --> 00:01:16,546
Work-stealing shines when task sizes are uneven or unpredictable.

20
00:01:16,546 --> 00:01:21,064
Too many tiny tasks add overhead — batch until splits are worthwhile.

21
00:01:21,064 --> 00:01:25,711
RecursiveTask of V extends ForkJoinTask — compute returns a value.

22
00:01:25,711 --> 00:01:30,006
Override compute — fork children, join them, combine results.

23
00:01:30,006 --> 00:01:34,089
Example — parallel sum of a large array by halving ranges.

24
00:01:34,089 --> 00:01:37,187
fork enqueues a subtask on the current pool.

25
00:01:37,187 --> 00:01:41,200
join blocks the current worker until the child completes.

26
00:01:41,200 --> 00:01:45,002
Use RecursiveTask when the result is a computed value.

27
00:01:45,002 --> 00:01:49,187
RecursiveAction extends ForkJoinTask of Void — side effects only.

28
00:01:49,187 --> 00:01:52,793
Override compute — fork subtasks, join, no return value.

29
00:01:52,793 --> 00:01:56,528
Example — parallel forEach over a tree or matrix in place.

30
00:01:56,528 --> 00:02:00,069
Same fork-join pattern — split work, wait for children.

31
00:02:00,069 --> 00:02:04,383
Choose RecursiveAction when you mutate shared structures carefully.

32
00:02:04,383 --> 00:02:07,860
Or when each leaf performs independent I/O or logging.

33
00:02:07,860 --> 00:02:09,476
When ForkJoinPool fits.

34
00:02:09,476 --> 00:02:14,395
CPU-bound divide-and-conquer — mergesort, matrix multiply, tree walks.

35
00:02:14,395 --> 00:02:18,049
Parallel streams use the common pool under the hood.

36
00:02:18,049 --> 00:02:21,352
Recursive decomposition with cheap merge steps.

37
00:02:21,352 --> 00:02:25,708
When not — many blocking I/O tasks — use a cached thread pool.

38
00:02:25,708 --> 00:02:29,854
When not — tiny uniform tasks — overhead beats parallelism.

39
00:02:29,854 --> 00:02:31,328
Three common mistakes.

40
00:02:31,328 --> 00:02:35,682
One — blocking inside compute — pins a worker and kills stealing.

41
00:02:35,682 --> 00:02:39,769
Two — shared mutable state without coordination — data races.

42
00:02:39,769 --> 00:02:44,056
Three — forking too deep — millions of tasks overwhelm the pool.

43
00:02:44,056 --> 00:02:48,679
Also — using commonPool for blocking work — starves parallel streams.

44
00:02:48,679 --> 00:02:52,832
Size tasks so fork overhead stays small relative to real work.

45
00:02:52,832 --> 00:02:56,071
Interview question — how does work-stealing work?

46
00:02:56,071 --> 00:02:59,442
Each thread has a deque — processes own tasks LIFO.

47
00:02:59,442 --> 00:03:03,870
Idle threads steal from the opposite end — FIFO — of another deque.

48
00:03:03,870 --> 00:03:07,902
Balances load without a global queue lock on every operation.

49
00:03:07,902 --> 00:03:12,462
RecursiveTask returns a value — RecursiveAction is void side effects.

50
00:03:12,462 --> 00:03:16,362
Mention parallel streams and CompletableFuture common pool.

51
00:03:16,362 --> 00:03:21,206
Pools share threads across tasks. What about per-thread state?

52
00:03:21,206 --> 00:03:23,863
Episode Forty-Eight — ThreadLocal.

53
00:03:23,863 --> 00:03:27,926
Per-thread variables, inheritance, and leak hazards.

54
00:03:27,926 --> 00:03:29,020
See you there.
